 "abstract": "This chapter is a historical overview of the development of the middle classes and their boys’ schools in England and Japan. In 19th-century England, these schools resisted any moves that might incorporate them into a system of ‘state-controlled’ or public education. Two individual grammar schools in the North of England are examined as cases of how these schools evolved at the institutional level. In Japan, the creation of a modern education system during the Meiji era is discussed with a focus on boys’ secondary schools. The dramatic introduction of co-education throughout the system after 1945 is then discussed. In spite of plans in both countries for the development of more equal societies, the historical legacies of gender norms and class identities turned out to be very enduring. The refusal of single-sex schools to go away – even in the state sector – is one manifestation of this."
}